From 59c61d3ce4c29b4b64b510f68bf4f1a778cec4ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anselm Busse <anselm.busse@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 05:37:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 38/49] x86_64/fpu/587: generic C for double and float where it
 is sound

Intel's 2011 x87 versions of acos, asin, acosh, atanh, exp, exp2, exp10,
log, log2, log10, log1p, expm1, pow, cbrt, logb, ilogb, significand,
remquo, frexp, cexp (double and float) lack the later underflow,
exception and accuracy fixes (acos was off by 140 ulps near 1, log(1)
was -0 when rounding downward).  The generic C versions work with the
x87 excess precision for these; rint, nearbyint, atan, atan2, asinh and
llrint stay x87 (the generic ones need exact double arithmetic), as do
all long double functions.  Double sqrt uses the C implementation: fsqrt
rounds twice for double results.  On mic0: 48 fewer libm test failures,
none new.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Anselm Busse <anselm.busse@outlook.com>
